        "Evolution of the Vertebrate Heart and Circulatory Pathways: Two-chambered branchial heart in fishes (one atrium, one ventricle) driving single circulation of deoxygenated blood to gills; Three-chambered heart in amphibians (two atria, one ventricle) with partial separation of systemic and pulmonary circuits (double incomplete circulation); Incompletely divided four-chambered heart in reptiles (interventricular septum complete in Crocodilia); Fully divided four-chambered heart in birds and mammals (two atria, two ventricles) ensuring complete anatomical separation of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood (double complete circulation).",
